# Intent: stopping a reply that is already playing (owner report 2026-10-21: tapping or saying "Hi, StackChan" while the robot speaks does not stop it). Three parts. (1) A tap while Speaking only told the host to abort; the host streams a whole reply faster than the unit plays it (438 frames were queued on the unit for a 34 s reply), so the unit kept playing from its own buffer. The tap now also drops the queued frames and goes idle. (2) Taps and touch events never reached the host during that reply, not even touch_down, so the touch path is now measured: the controller poll (count, failures, longest gap) and the display read (count, longest gap), reported once a minute as touch_path. A long gap or failures while it speaks would show the touch is starved. (3) The wake word was armed while speaking (armed=1, fetches continuing) but did not fire; wake_stats gains vad_speech, the number of audio chunks the front end heard as speech, to show whether it hears the person over the reply at all.
# Upstream: 78/xiaozhi-esp32 main/application.cc, main/audio/wake_words/afe_wake_word.cc; m5stack/StackChan firmware/main/hal/hal.cpp, hal/hal_head_touch.cpp, hal/board/stackchan.cc, hal/device_events.h (MIT). Applies after 060 with --unidiff-zero.
# Check after a flash: tap the face while it speaks a long reply: "unit tap: decision=stop state=6 why=speaking" and the voice stops at once. Each minute "unit touch: touch_path polls=~3000 poll_fail=0 poll_gap_max_ms=... reads=... read_gap_max_ms=..." and "unit wake: wake_stats ... vad_speech=N". Say "Hi, StackChan" over a long reply: a "unit wake: wake state=6" line and the reply stops, or vad_speech stays near zero in that minute.
